#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:5
msgid "Entry Points"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:7
msgid ""
"Entry points are a type of metadata that can be exposed by packages on "
"installation. They are a very useful feature of the Python ecosystem, and "
"come specially handy in two scenarios:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:11
msgid ""
"1. The package would like to provide commands to be run at the terminal. "
"This functionality is known as *console* scripts. The command may also open "
"up a GUI, in which case it is known as a *GUI* script. An example of a "
"console script is the one provided by the :pypi:`pip` package, which allows "
"you to run commands like ``pip install`` in the terminal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:17
msgid ""
"2. A package would like to enable customization of its functionalities via "
"*plugins*. For example, the test framework :pypi:`pytest` allows "
"customization via the ``pytest11`` entry point, and the syntax highlighting "
"tool :pypi:`pygments` allows specifying additional styles using the entry "
"point ``pygments.styles``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:27
msgid "Console Scripts"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:29
msgid ""
"Let us start with console scripts. First consider an example without entry "
"points. Imagine a package defined thus::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:33
msgid ""
"project_root_directory\n"
"├── pyproject.toml        # and/or setup.cfg, setup.py\n"
"└── src\n"
"    └── timmins\n"
"        ├── __init__.py\n"
"        └── ..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:40
msgid "with ``__init__.py`` as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:42
msgid ""
"def hello_world():\n"
"    print(\"Hello world\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:47
msgid ""
"Now, suppose that we would like to provide some way of executing the "
"function ``hello_world()`` from the command-line. One way to do this is to "
"create a file ``src/timmins/__main__.py`` providing a hook as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:52
msgid ""
"from . import hello_world\n"
"\n"
"if __name__ == '__main__':\n"
"    hello_world()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:59
msgid ""
"Then, after installing the package ``timmins``, we may invoke the "
"``hello_world()`` function as follows, through the `runpy <https://docs."
"python.org/3/library/runpy.html>`_ module:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:63
msgid ""
"$ python -m timmins\n"
"Hello world"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:68
msgid ""
"Instead of this approach using ``__main__.py``, you can also create a user-"
"friendly CLI executable that can be called directly without ``python -m``. "
"In the above example, to create a command ``hello-world`` that invokes "
"``timmins.hello_world``, add a console script entry point to your "
"configuration:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:76
msgid ""
"[project.scripts]\n"
"hello-world = \"timmins:hello_world\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:83
msgid ""
"[options.entry_points]\n"
"console_scripts =\n"
"    hello-world = timmins:hello_world"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:91
msgid ""
"from setuptools import setup\n"
"\n"
"setup(\n"
"    # ...,\n"
"    entry_points={\n"
"        'console_scripts': [\n"
"            'hello-world = timmins:hello_world',\n"
"        ]\n"
"    }\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:105
msgid ""
"After installing the package, a user may invoke that function by simply "
"calling ``hello-world`` on the command line:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:108
msgid ""
"$ hello-world\n"
"Hello world"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:113
msgid ""
"Note that any function used as a console script, i.e. ``hello_world()`` in "
"this example, should not accept any arguments. If your function requires any "
"input from the user, you can use regular command-line argument parsing "
"utilities like :mod:`argparse` within the body of the function to parse user "
"input given via :obj:`sys.argv`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:119
msgid ""
"You may have noticed that we have used a special syntax to specify the "
"function that must be invoked by the console script, i.e. we have written "
"``timmins:hello_world`` with a colon ``:`` separating the package name and "
"the function name. The full specification of this syntax is discussed in the "
"`last section <#entry-points-syntax>`_ of this document, and this can be "
"used to specify a function located anywhere in your package, not just in "
"``__init__.py``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:127
msgid "GUI Scripts"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:129
msgid ""
"In addition to ``console_scripts``, Setuptools supports ``gui_scripts``, "
"which will launch a GUI application without running in a terminal window."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:132
msgid ""
"For example, if we have a project with the same directory structure as "
"before, with an ``__init__.py`` file containing the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:135
msgid ""
"import PySimpleGUI as sg\n"
"\n"
"def hello_world():\n"
"    sg.Window(title=\"Hello world\", layout=[[]], margins=(100, 50)).read()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:142
msgid "Then, we can add a GUI script entry point:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:146
msgid ""
"[project.gui-scripts]\n"
"hello-world = \"timmins:hello_world\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:153
msgid ""
"[options.entry_points]\n"
"gui_scripts =\n"
"    hello-world = timmins:hello_world"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:161
msgid ""
"from setuptools import setup\n"
"\n"
"setup(\n"
"    # ...,\n"
"    entry_points={\n"
"        'gui_scripts': [\n"
"            'hello-world = timmins:hello_world',\n"
"        ]\n"
"    }\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:175
msgid ""
"To be able to import ``PySimpleGUI``, you need to add ``pysimplegui`` to "
"your package dependencies. See :doc:`/userguide/dependency_management` for "
"more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:178
msgid "Now, running:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:180
msgid "$ hello-world"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:184
msgid "will open a small application window with the title 'Hello world'."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:186
msgid ""
"Note that just as with console scripts, any function used as a GUI script "
"should not accept any arguments, and any user input can be parsed within the "
"body of the function. GUI scripts also use the same syntax (discussed in the "
"`last section <#entry-points-syntax>`_) for specifying the function to be "
"invoked."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:193
msgid ""
"The difference between ``console_scripts`` and ``gui_scripts`` only affects "
"Windows systems. [#use_for_scripts]_ ``console_scripts`` are wrapped in a "
"console executable, so they are attached to a console and can use ``sys."
"stdin``, ``sys.stdout`` and ``sys.stderr`` for input and output. "
"``gui_scripts`` are wrapped in a GUI executable, so they can be started "
"without a console, but cannot use standard streams unless application code "
"redirects them. Other platforms do not have the same distinction."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:203
msgid ""
"Console and GUI scripts work because behind the scenes, installers like :"
"pypi:`pip` create wrapper scripts around the function(s) being invoked. For "
"example, the ``hello-world`` entry point in the above two examples would "
"create a command ``hello-world`` launching a script like this: "
"[#use_for_scripts]_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:208
msgid ""
"import sys\n"
"from timmins import hello_world\n"
"sys.exit(hello_world())"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:217
msgid "Advertising Behavior"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:219
msgid ""
"Console/GUI scripts are one use of the more general concept of entry points. "
"Entry points more generally allow a packager to advertise behavior for "
"discovery by other libraries and applications. This feature enables \"plug-"
"in\"-like functionality, where one library solicits entry points and any "
"number of other libraries provide those entry points."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:225
msgid ""
"A good example of this plug-in behavior can be seen in `pytest plugins "
"<https://docs.pytest.org/en/latest/writing_plugins.html>`_, where pytest is "
"a test framework that allows other libraries to extend or modify its "
"functionality through the ``pytest11`` entry point."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:230
msgid ""
"The console/GUI scripts work similarly, where libraries advertise their "
"commands and tools like ``pip`` create wrapper scripts that invoke those "
"commands."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:234
msgid "Entry Points for Plugins"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:236
msgid ""
"Let us consider a simple example to understand how we can implement entry "
"points corresponding to plugins. Say we have a package ``timmins`` with the "
"following directory structure::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:240
msgid ""
"timmins\n"
"├── pyproject.toml        # and/or setup.cfg, setup.py\n"
"└── src\n"
"    └── timmins\n"
"        └── __init__.py"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:246
msgid "and in ``src/timmins/__init__.py`` we have the following code:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:248
msgid ""
"def hello_world():\n"
"    print('Hello world')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:253
msgid ""
"Basically, we have defined a ``hello_world()`` function which will print the "
"text 'Hello world'. Now, let us say we want to print the text 'Hello world' "
"in different ways. The current function just prints the text as it is - let "
"us say we want another style in which the text is enclosed within "
"exclamation marks::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:258
msgid "!!! Hello world !!!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:260
msgid ""
"Let us see how this can be done using plugins. First, let us separate the "
"style of printing the text from the text itself. In other words, we can "
"change the code in ``src/timmins/__init__.py`` to something like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:264
msgid ""
"def display(text):\n"
"    print(text)\n"
"\n"
"def hello_world():\n"
"    display('Hello world')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:272
msgid ""
"Here, the ``display()`` function controls the style of printing the text, "
"and the ``hello_world()`` function calls the ``display()`` function to print "
"the text 'Hello world`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:276
msgid ""
"Right now the ``display()`` function just prints the text as it is. In order "
"to be able to customize it, we can do the following. Let us introduce a new "
"*group* of entry points named ``timmins.display``, and expect plugin "
"packages implementing this entry point to supply a ``display()``-like "
"function. Next, to be able to automatically discover plugin packages that "
"implement this entry point, we can use the :mod:`importlib.metadata` module, "
"as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:284
msgid ""
"from importlib.metadata import entry_points\n"
"display_eps = entry_points(group='timmins.display')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:290
msgid ""
"Each ``importlib.metadata.EntryPoint`` object is an object containing a "
"``name``, a ``group``, and a ``value``. For example, after setting up the "
"plugin package as described below, ``display_eps`` in the above code will "
"look like this: [#package_metadata]_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:294
msgid ""
"(\n"
"    EntryPoint(name='excl', value='timmins_plugin_fancy:excl_display', "
"group='timmins.display'),\n"
"    ...,\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:301
msgid ""
"``display_eps`` will now be a list of ``EntryPoint`` objects, each referring "
"to ``display()``-like functions defined by one or more installed plugin "
"packages. Then, to import a specific ``display()``-like function - let us "
"choose the one corresponding to the first discovered entry point - we can "
"use the ``load()`` method as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:306
msgid "display = display_eps[0].load()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:310
msgid ""
"Finally, a sensible behaviour would be that if we cannot find any plugin "
"packages customizing the ``display()`` function, we should fall back to our "
"default implementation which prints the text as it is. With this behaviour "
"included, the code in ``src/timmins/__init__.py`` finally becomes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:315
msgid ""
"from importlib.metadata import entry_points\n"
"display_eps = entry_points(group='timmins.display')\n"
"try:\n"
"    display = display_eps[0].load()\n"
"except IndexError:\n"
"    def display(text):\n"
"        print(text)\n"
"\n"
"def hello_world():\n"
"    display('Hello world')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:328
msgid ""
"That finishes the setup on ``timmins``'s side. Next, we need to implement a "
"plugin which implements the entry point ``timmins.display``. Let us name "
"this plugin ``timmins-plugin-fancy``, and set it up with the following "
"directory structure::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:332
msgid ""
"timmins-plugin-fancy\n"
"├── pyproject.toml        # and/or setup.cfg, setup.py\n"
"└── src\n"
"    └── timmins_plugin_fancy\n"
"        └── __init__.py"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:338
msgid ""
"And then, inside ``src/timmins_plugin_fancy/__init__.py``, we can put a "
"function named ``excl_display()`` that prints the given text surrounded by "
"exclamation marks:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:341
msgid ""
"def excl_display(text):\n"
"    print('!!!', text, '!!!')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:346
msgid ""
"This is the ``display()``-like function that we are looking to supply to the "
"``timmins`` package. We can do that by adding the following in the "
"configuration of ``timmins-plugin-fancy``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:352
msgid ""
"# Note the quotes around timmins.display in order to escape the dot .\n"
"[project.entry-points.\"timmins.display\"]\n"
"excl = \"timmins_plugin_fancy:excl_display\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:360
msgid ""
"[options.entry_points]\n"
"timmins.display =\n"
"    excl = timmins_plugin_fancy:excl_display"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:368
msgid ""
"from setuptools import setup\n"
"\n"
"setup(\n"
"    # ...,\n"
"    entry_points = {\n"
"        'timmins.display': [\n"
"            'excl = timmins_plugin_fancy:excl_display'\n"
"        ]\n"
"    }\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:381
msgid ""
"Basically, this configuration states that we are a supplying an entry point "
"under the group ``timmins.display``. The entry point is named ``excl`` and "
"it refers to the function ``excl_display`` defined by the package ``timmins-"
"plugin-fancy``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:385
msgid ""
"Now, if we install both ``timmins`` and ``timmins-plugin-fancy``, we should "
"get the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:388
msgid ""
">>> from timmins import hello_world\n"
">>> hello_world()\n"
"!!! Hello world !!!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:394
msgid ""
"whereas if we only install ``timmins`` and not ``timmins-plugin-fancy``, we "
"should get the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:397
msgid ""
">>> from timmins import hello_world\n"
">>> hello_world()\n"
"Hello world"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:403
msgid "Therefore, our plugin works."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:405
msgid ""
"Our plugin could have also defined multiple entry points under the group "
"``timmins.display``. For example, in ``src/timmins_plugin_fancy/__init__."
"py`` we could have two ``display()``-like functions, as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:409
msgid ""
"def excl_display(text):\n"
"    print('!!!', text, '!!!')\n"
"\n"
"def lined_display(text):\n"
"    print(''.join(['-' for _ in text]))\n"
"    print(text)\n"
"    print(''.join(['-' for _ in text]))"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:419
msgid "The configuration of ``timmins-plugin-fancy`` would then change to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:423
msgid ""
"[project.entry-points.\"timmins.display\"]\n"
"excl = \"timmins_plugin_fancy:excl_display\"\n"
"lined = \"timmins_plugin_fancy:lined_display\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:431
msgid ""
"[options.entry_points]\n"
"timmins.display =\n"
"    excl = timmins_plugin_fancy:excl_display\n"
"    lined = timmins_plugin_fancy:lined_display"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:440
msgid ""
"from setuptools import setup\n"
"\n"
"setup(\n"
"    # ...,\n"
"    entry_points = {\n"
"        'timmins.display': [\n"
"            'excl = timmins_plugin_fancy:excl_display',\n"
"            'lined = timmins_plugin_fancy:lined_display',\n"
"        ]\n"
"    }\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:454
msgid ""
"On the ``timmins`` side, we can also use a different strategy of loading "
"entry points. For example, we can search for a specific display style:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:457
msgid ""
"display_eps = entry_points(group='timmins.display')\n"
"try:\n"
"    display = display_eps['lined'].load()\n"
"except KeyError:\n"
"    # if the 'lined' display is not available, use something else\n"
"    ..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:466
msgid ""
"Or we can also load all plugins under the given group. Though this might not "
"be of much use in our current example, there are several scenarios in which "
"this is useful:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:470
msgid ""
"display_eps = entry_points(group='timmins.display')\n"
"for ep in display_eps:\n"
"    display = ep.load()\n"
"    # do something with display\n"
"    ..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:478
msgid ""
"Another point is that in this particular example, we have used plugins to "
"customize the behaviour of a function (``display()``). In general, we can "
"use entry points to enable plugins to not only customize the behaviour of "
"functions, but also of entire classes and modules. This is unlike the case "
"of console/GUI scripts, where entry points can only refer to functions. The "
"syntax used for specifying the entry points remains the same as for console/"
"GUI scripts, and is discussed in the `last section <#entry-points-syntax>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:487
msgid ""
"The recommended approach for loading and importing entry points is the :mod:"
"`importlib.metadata` module, which is a part of the standard library since "
"Python 3.8 and is non-provisional since Python 3.10. For older versions of "
"Python, its backport :pypi:`importlib_metadata` should be used. While using "
"the backport, the only change that has to be made is to replace ``importlib."
"metadata`` with ``importlib_metadata``, i.e."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:495
msgid ""
"from importlib_metadata import entry_points\n"
"..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:500
msgid ""
"In summary, entry points allow a package to open its functionalities for "
"customization via plugins. The package soliciting the entry points need not "
"have any dependency or prior knowledge about the plugins implementing the "
"entry points, and downstream users are able to compose functionality by "
"pulling together plugins implementing the entry points."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:508
msgid "Entry Points Syntax"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:510
msgid "The syntax for entry points is specified as follows::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:512
msgid "<name> = <package_or_module>[:<object>[.<attr>[.<nested-attr>]*]]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:514
msgid ""
"Here, the square brackets ``[]`` denote optionality and the asterisk ``*`` "
"denotes repetition. ``name`` is the name of the script/entry point you want "
"to create, the left hand side of ``:`` is the package or module that "
"contains the object you want to invoke (think about it as something you "
"would write in an import statement), and the right hand side is the object "
"you want to invoke (e.g. a function)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:521
msgid "To make this syntax more clear, consider the following examples:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:523
msgid "Package or module"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:524
#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:538
#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:551
msgid "If you supply::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:526
msgid "<name> = <package_or_module>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:528
msgid ""
"as the entry point, where ``<package_or_module>`` can contain ``.`` in the "
"case of sub-modules or sub-packages, then, tools in the Python ecosystem "
"will roughly interpret this value as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:532
msgid ""
"import <package_or_module>\n"
"parsed_value = <package_or_module>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:537
msgid "Module-level object"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:540
msgid "<name> = <package_or_module>:<object>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:542
msgid ""
"where ``<object>`` does not contain any ``.``, this will be roughly "
"interpreted as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:545
msgid ""
"from <package_or_module> import <object>\n"
"parsed_value = <object>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:550
msgid "Nested object"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:553
msgid "<name> = <package_or_module>:<object>.<attr>.<nested_attr>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:555
msgid "this will be roughly interpreted as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:557
msgid ""
"from <package_or_module> import <object>\n"
"parsed_value = <object>.<attr>.<nested_attr>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/entry_point.rst:562
msgid ""
